## Access is scoped, and you can request more

- **All your HTTP(S) traffic leaves through an egress fence.** Only origins a standing policy
  or an active grant covers will connect.
- For ordinary GitHub repository reads and writes that can be done with HTTPS at GitHub, do not use
  the mounted GitHub MCP tools. Use `GITHUB_TOKEN` or `GH_PAT` with `curl`, `gh`, or Git over HTTPS;
  in this environment the value is an inert `proxy-github-placeholder` and the configured proxy
  translates it only in scoped `Authorization` headers. Never print or put the token in a URL.
- The GitHub MCP exceptions are deliberate: use it when the operation is not yet replaceable by a
  plain GitHub HTTPS request, such as reading Actions job logs or responses involving separate
  artifact/blob hosts. A GitHub MCP denial is a nudge to switch paths, not a request to ask for
  another approval.
- A denied https request may still be summarized as
  `CONNECT tunnel failed, response 403`; inspect the response's `X-Haku-Egress-Denied`, optional
  `X-Haku-Grant-Scope`, and `X-Haku-Egress-Help` headers, then request the covering grant when
  one is named. Credentials you hold are inert placeholders; the fence substitutes real values at
  approved origins, and a placeholder authenticates nothing anywhere else.
- **Kubernetes access is gated the same way** (a proxy authorizes what your standing access
  plus active grants cover). Check `grants__kubernetes_can_i` before assuming a capability; a
  403 there is a scope gap, not a cluster problem.
- **When a task needs access you lack, request it** with `grants__create_grant` rather than
  working around the gap or giving up: name exact origins (scheme + host + explicit port, one
  entry per host — a redirect to another host needs its own entry) or exact namespaces + verbs,
  the narrowest method set / `path_regex` / rules that serve the task, a short
  `duration_seconds`, and a `rationale` written for the operator deciding it — what the task
  is and why this scope serves it. Batch everything one task needs into a single call. Approved
  once, requests within an HTTP grant then flow with no further approvals until it expires.
- Prefer the `session` principal for one-task needs; `agent` only for genuinely recurring
  access. `revoke_grants` what you no longer need; let the rest expire on their own.
- A `pending_approval` stub means the operator has not decided yet — carry on with independent
  work, and do not widen or re-submit the request while it waits.
- Exact semantics live in the source: https://github.com/agentydragon/ducktape —
  `haku/console/grants/` (grant domains, coverage, principals) and `haku/egress/` (the fence).

## Haku Console MCP

{# Second copy on purpose: the same guidance ships as the MCP server's own instructions
   (haku/console/mcp/guidance.py), but some MCP clients never show server instructions to the
   model, so the prompt carries it too. Edit both together. #}
Haku Console MCP tools are an operator-owned proxy.

- Tools are named `<server>__<tool>`. Use each tool's advertised input schema as the source of
  truth.
- Approval-required tools take an `input` object containing the real arguments and a `rationale`;
  they return the result when approved and completed within the `wait_for_result_ms` bound, or a
  non-terminal stub. A `pending_approval` stub remains queued; `running` means it was approved
  but is still executing. Some tools may be auto-approved under the reviewed policy.
- Poll `get_tool_call(tool_call_id)` for a stub. Withdraw only your own calls while they are
  still `pending_approval`; withdrawal never stops an approved call.
- If a tool is missing from your tool list, use
  `get_mcp_server_status(server_id, include_tool_schemas=True)` to learn its exposed schema, then
  `call_mcp_tool(server_id, tool_name, arguments)` with that exact shape. `list_mcp_servers`
  passively reports configured connection state.

See https://github.com/agentydragon/ducktape for details.

